# Non-Null Parameter Contract

When a reference type (class, interface) is passed to a method or constructor, the **callee assumes it is not null**. Do not add defensive null checks on that parameter inside the callee.

## Callees: use the parameter directly

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — hides a caller bug
public void ApplyEffect(Unit target, Effect effect)
{
    if (target == null)
    {
        return;
    }
    target.Apply(effect);
}

// ✅ GOOD — trust the contract
public void ApplyEffect(Unit target, Effect effect)
{
    target.Apply(effect);
}
```

No `if (x == null) return`, no log-then-return, no `?.` on a required parameter. If null is impossible, **do not check**.

## Boundaries: validate once, fail fast

Validate only where values enter the system — then never re-check downstream.

| Boundary | Pattern |
|----------|---------|
| Constructor / init with required dep | `dep ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(dep))` |
| `[SerializeField]` ref in `Awake` | `this.Require(_field, nameof(_field))` — not a hand-rolled check, not silent skip |
| Network / deserialize / user input | Validate in the handler; inner helpers assume non-null |

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD — boundary throws; every callee after this trusts _registry
public UnitRegistry(IReadOnlyDictionary<int, Unit> units)
{
    _units = units ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(units));
}
```

## Serialized fields: validate once in Awake, then trust

The same contract applies to `[SerializeField]` references — the boundary is `Awake`.

- **A `[SerializeField]` reference used unguarded anywhere in the class is required**: validate it once in `Awake` with the shared `this.Require` extension (see the required-serialized-refs rule — it throws and disables the component), then use it directly everywhere. No `if (_field != null)` or `_field?.` on a field that some other method already dereferences bare — that's the worst of both worlds: it still NREs on the unguarded path, and it hides the misconfiguration on the guarded one.

```csharp
private void Awake()
{
    this.Require(_hudController, nameof(_hudController));
}
```

- **Don't create maybe-null fields via lazy resolution.** A field populated on demand by `Ensure*()` / `FindFirstObjectByType` / `GetComponent` fallbacks forces every method to consider its nullness — that pattern is where scattered null checks come from. Resolve dependencies at a boundary (`Awake` or an explicit `Init`) and fail loudly if missing. If a ref is genuinely optional, say so with a comment at the field and check it at each feature entry point, not defensively everywhere.

## Downstream code trusts boundary-validated values

Once a value passes its boundary check, everything after it uses the value directly. A handler that validated a payload does not hand the maybe-null onward for helpers to re-check:

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD — handler is the boundary; helpers trust their parameters
private void HandleTurnChange(TableUpdate update)
{
    var data = update.GetData<TurnChangeData>();
    if (data == null)
    {
        Debug.LogError($"[Game] HandleTurnChange — payload deserialized to null (seq={update.ServerSeq})");
        return;
    }
    ApplyTurn(data);          // ApplyTurn does NOT re-check data
}

// ❌ BAD — ApplyTurn re-checks what the handler already guaranteed
private void ApplyTurn(TurnChangeData data)
{
    if (data == null) return;
    ...
}
```

## When null is a real possibility

Express it in the API — do not use a plain reference type plus a silent null check.

- `TryGetUnit(int id, out Unit unit)` for may-be-absent
- `Unit?` / explicit nullable only when absence is normal
- Separate methods for optional vs required paths

## Allowed exceptions (narrow)

- **Legitimately optional serialized fields** checked once in `Awake`, not in every method
- **`TryGetComponent` / lookup APIs** where absence is the expected outcome (see component-access rule)

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No new `if (param == null) return` on reference-type **parameters** in callees
- [ ] Required deps validated once at boundary with throw (`this.Require` for serialized refs), not silent return
- [ ] `[SerializeField]` refs used unguarded anywhere are Awake-validated and never null-checked elsewhere
- [ ] No lazy `Ensure*` / `FindFirstObjectByType` field resolution producing maybe-null fields
- [ ] Optional values use `Try*`, nullable types, or explicit optional APIs — not defensive checks on plain refs
